    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Every driveway sealer failure we have been called to look at in the last decade traces back to one of three things: bad prep, too much water in the bucket, or the wrong weather. Weather is the one people think they have checked and have not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="the-window" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          1. The Real Temperature Window
        </h2>

        <p>
          Read the back of a pail of Latex-ite, Black Jack or any big-box asphalt emulsion and you will find the same line: apply when air and surface temperatures are 50°F and rising, and stay above 50°F for 24 hours. That is the legal minimum, written by a chemist who wants the product to survive on a driveway where the owner did everything else right. It is not the target.
        </p>

        <p>
          The window we actually work to is <strong>55 to 85°F air temperature, with the pavement somewhere between 60 and 110°F</strong>, relative humidity under about 65 per cent, a light breeze, and no rain in the forecast for a full day. Inside that window a water-based sealer flows out evenly, loses its water at a steady rate, and the asphalt or coal tar particles have time to knit together into a continuous film before the surface skins over. Outside it, one of two things happens: the water leaves too fast and the film cracks and streaks, or the water leaves too slowly and the emulsion never coalesces properly.
        </p>

        <p>
          The 50°F minimum is a chemistry threshold, not a comfort threshold. Asphalt emulsion is tiny droplets of asphalt suspended in water with a soap-like emulsifier holding them apart. As the water evaporates the droplets crowd together and eventually fuse. That fusion, called coalescence, needs the asphalt to be soft enough to flow into its neighbours, and asphalt at 45°F is not soft. You can get a coat that looks dry on a cool day and is still a pile of loose particles sitting on the pavement, which is why sealer applied in a cool October week powders off under the first snowplough.
        </p>

        <p>
          At the top of the window the problem flips. Above about 85°F air temperature in full sun the pavement is hot enough to boil the water out of the film faster than the sealer can level, and above 95°F we simply do not apply. The section on hot weather below covers how to stretch that limit when you have no choice.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A quick summary of the numbers</h3>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Condition</th><th>Minimum</th><th>Ideal</th><th>Stop</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Air temperature at application</td><td>50°F and rising</td><td>55 to 85°F</td><td>Above 95°F or below 50°F</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Pavement surface temperature</td><td>50°F</td><td>60 to 110°F</td><td>Above 130°F</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Overnight low, first 24 hours</td><td>Above 45°F</td><td>Above 55°F</td><td>Anything near 40°F</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Relative humidity</td><td>Under 85 per cent</td><td>Under 65 per cent</td><td>Fog, drizzle or dew forming</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Wind</td><td>Calm to light</td><td>3 to 10 mph</td><td>Gusting above 20 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Dry time before rain</td><td>8 hours</td><td>24 hours</td><td>Rain within 6 hours</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          Those are the figures for the water-based asphalt emulsion and coal tar sealers that make up nearly everything sold at Home Depot, Lowe's and Menards. Solvent-based and acrylic products behave a little differently and we note where below. If you are choosing between them, our <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-driveway-sealer/">oil-based vs water-based driveway sealer</a> comparison covers the trade-offs.
        </p>

        <h2 id="surface-vs-air" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          2. Surface Temperature vs Air Temperature
        </h2>

        <p>
          The forecast on your phone tells you the air temperature at roughly five feet above grass, in the shade, at the nearest airport. Your sealer does not cure at the airport. It cures on a sheet of black asphalt that absorbs nearly all the sunlight that hits it, and that sheet can be a completely different temperature.
        </p>

        <p>
          On a clear July afternoon with the air at 88°F, we routinely measure asphalt at 125 to 135°F. On a sunny 75°F day it will be 100 to 110°F. The rule of thumb is that <strong>black pavement in direct sun runs 20 to 40°F hotter than the air</strong>, with the gap widest when the sun is high and the wind is still. That is why an "ideal" 80°F afternoon can produce flash-drying and lap marks if you apply at two o'clock, while the same day is perfect at eight in the morning.
        </p>

        <p>
          It works the other way too. A driveway that sits in the shade of the house all morning, or one with a north-facing slope, can be 10°F cooler than the air on a spring day. The bucket says 50°F and rising; the air is 54°F; the shaded pavement is 46°F and holding cold from the night before. That coat is going on below the minimum whatever the forecast said, and it is the single most common way people seal in April and regret it in June.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Measure it, do not guess</h3>

        <p>
          A cheap infrared thermometer, the kind sold for checking grill temperatures or car brakes, costs less than a pail of sealer and takes the guesswork out entirely. Point it at the pavement in three or four places, including the shadiest corner and the spot that gets the most sun, and note the range. If the coldest reading is above 55°F and the hottest is under 110°F, you are in good shape. If the spread is wide, plan to work the cool sections when the sun reaches them and the hot sections before it does.
          Without a thermometer, the back of your hand is a rough guide: pavement that feels pleasantly warm is in range; pavement that is too hot to hold your palm on for five seconds is over 120°F and needs to cool or be misted.
        </p>

        <p>
          Surface temperature also depends on what came before. A driveway that was rained on the previous day and is still dark with moisture will read cool because evaporation is pulling heat out of it. That is a double problem: cold surface, and residual moisture that will thin the sealer from below. Our guide on <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a> covers what happens when water meets a fresh coat, and the same chemistry applies to water that was already in the pavement.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overnight" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          3. The Overnight Lows That Matter
        </h2>

        <p>
          The daytime temperature gets the sealer dry to the touch. The overnight temperature decides whether it cures. Most homeowners check the high for the day they plan to seal and never look at the low, and that is exactly backwards for anything applied after mid-September.
        </p>

        <p>
          Dry to touch happens within two to four hours in good conditions. Full cure, where the film has enough strength to take tyres and resist water, takes 24 to 48 hours and continues slowly for a week or more. During that first night the film is still soft and still contains water, and if the temperature falls into the mid-40s the coalescence process slows to almost nothing. It picks up again the next morning if the day is warm, so one cool night after a hot sunny day usually gets away with it. Two cool nights in a row with a cloudy day between them do not.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our rule: <strong>the low for the first night should be above 50°F, and the low for the second night should not drop below 45°F.</strong> If the forecast shows the first night at 42°F, wait. The sealer is not going anywhere in the pail and the driveway will still be there next week.
        </p>

        <p>
          The other overnight issue is dew. When the surface cools below the dew point, water condenses on it, and on a film that is dry to the touch but not cured that water can rewet the surface, leach out emulsifier and leave a greyish, blotchy finish that looks like the sealer was diluted. It usually wears off within a few weeks of traffic, but on a shaded driveway with heavy dew it can persist. The fix is timing: seal in the morning so the film has the maximum number of warm daylight hours before the first dew falls.
        </p>

        <h2 id="humidity-wind-sun" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          4. Humidity, Dew Point, Wind and Sun
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Humidity</h3>

        <p>
          A water-based sealer dries by giving up water to the air. The drier the air, the faster it takes that water. At 40 per cent relative humidity and 75°F a coat of asphalt emulsion is walkable in about two hours. At 80 per cent humidity the same coat can take six to eight hours, and if the humidity stays high overnight it may not be properly dry the next morning. In the humid Southeast and Gulf Coast in August this is the limiting factor rather than temperature, which is why contractors there start at dawn and knock off before lunch.
        </p>

        <p>
          Humidity also decides how much sealer you can put down at once. A thick single coat in humid weather traps water under a skinned-over surface and stays soft for days. Two thin coats, each given time to dry, is the answer in sticky weather, and it is generally the better approach anyway, as we explain in <a href="/how-many-coats-of-driveway-sealer/">how many coats of driveway sealer</a> you actually need.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dew point</h3>

        <p>
          Dew point is the more useful number, and most weather apps show it if you scroll. It tells you the temperature at which the air is saturated and water starts condensing on cool surfaces. If the overnight low is within about 5°F of the dew point, the driveway will be wet with dew by morning. If the dew point is above 65°F, the air is holding so much water that drying will be slow all day regardless of the temperature. A dew point in the 40s or 50s with an afternoon high in the 70s is close to perfect sealing weather.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Wind</h3>

        <p>
          A light breeze is your friend. It moves the saturated layer of air off the surface and lets fresh, drier air take its place, which is why a 5 to 10 mph wind can cut drying time in half. Too much wind is a different matter. Above about 20 mph a spray application drifts onto the neighbour's car and the grass, and even a squeegee coat skins over so fast on the windward edge that lap marks become inevitable. Wind also carries dust, leaves and seed fluff onto the wet film, and every bit of it becomes a permanent part of the finish.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sun</h3>

        <p>
          Sun is what heats the pavement, and it is what drives the cure. A fully overcast day at 65°F is a slower cure than a sunny day at 60°F, because the surface never warms up. Bright sun is desirable in spring and autumn and something to manage in midsummer, when it pushes the pavement past the point where the sealer can level before it dries. Partial cloud in July, with a surface in the 90s, is about as good as sealing weather gets.
        </p>

        <h2 id="hot-weather" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          5. Sealing in Hot Weather
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sometimes the only weekend you have is in the middle of a heatwave. Hot weather sealing is possible and pros do it all summer, but the failure mode is specific and you need to work around it. When sealer hits pavement at 120°F or more, the water in the film flashes off in seconds. The film skins over before it has levelled, so squeegee marks and brush strokes freeze in place, the leading edge of each pass dries before the next pass overlaps it, and you get visible lap lines and a finish that looks streaky the moment it dries. The film underneath is also weaker, because the asphalt particles were pushed together by evaporation rather than given time to flow.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Work in the morning</h3>

        <p>
          Start as soon as the dew has burned off, typically between seven and nine, and aim to finish the last pass before the pavement climbs over 110°F. On a 90°F day that usually means done by eleven. Do not plan to seal a 1,000 square foot driveway in the afternoon in July; it will not go well and there is no product on the shelf that changes that.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mist the surface first</h3>

        <p>
          This is the pro trick that makes hot-weather sealing workable. A light fog of water from a garden hose on a mist setting, applied just ahead of the sealer, cools the pavement by 15 to 25°F and slows the initial flash so the coat can level. The key word is light: you want the pavement damp and dark with no standing water, no puddles and no wet patches in the low spots. Standing water thins the sealer and leaves pale patches. If you see puddles, push them off with a squeegee and wait a minute before applying.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Follow the shade</h3>

        <p>
          If the house or the trees shade part of the driveway, start with the section that is in the sun now and will be shaded later, or the reverse, depending on the timing. Work in strips that you can complete in one go, keep a wet edge, and never leave a strip half done while you go and refill the bucket. Have the next pail open and stirred before you need it.
        </p>

        <p>
          On a very hot day, thin the first coat slightly if the manufacturer allows it (typically no more than 10 per cent water for a first coat on rough asphalt) and apply two thinner coats rather than one heavy one. The second coat goes on the next morning, not the same afternoon, so the first has a full cycle to cure.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          6. Why Cold Kills an Emulsion
        </h2>

        <p>
          Cold does not just slow a water-based sealer down; below a certain point it destroys it. Understanding why explains every autumn sealing failure we have been asked to diagnose.
        </p>

        <p>
          An emulsion is a suspension held together by chemistry that assumes liquid water. When the film starts to freeze, ice crystals form and push the asphalt droplets together in clumps rather than a continuous sheet. The emulsifier is disrupted, the droplets fuse randomly, and what you get after thawing is a crumbly, grey, matte residue that has no adhesion and no film strength. The same thing happens to a pail of sealer left in an unheated garage over winter, which is why the labels say keep from freezing. Once an emulsion has frozen it is scrap, in the bucket or on the ground.
        </p>

        <p>
          Short of freezing, low temperatures cause a subtler failure. Between roughly 40 and 50°F the water still evaporates, slowly, but the asphalt is too stiff to coalesce properly. The coat dries to something that looks acceptable and passes a fingertip test, but it has poor cohesion. It powders under tyres, tracks into the garage, and lifts in sheets the following spring. Homeowners see this and assume the product was faulty or the driveway was dirty. Sometimes it was, but on the driveways we have looked at, the application date is usually late October in a northern state.
        </p>

        <p>
          Coal tar emulsions are slightly more tolerant of cool weather than asphalt emulsions, and solvent-based sealers, which cure by evaporation of a petroleum carrier rather than water, can be applied down to about 40°F. Acrylic sealers sit in between. None of them survive a freeze before cure. Our piece on <a href="/can-you-seal-a-driveway-in-cold-weather/">sealing a driveway in cold weather</a> goes into the product-by-product limits.
        </p>

        <h2 id="regions" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          7. Seasonal Timing by US Region
        </h2>

        <p>
          The general rule is late May through September, but the country is big and the good windows shift by a month or more from one region to the next. These are the windows we would work to.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Northeast and Upper Midwest</h3>

        <p>
          Memorial Day to mid-September, with the sweet spot in June and the first half of September. Nights in May can still fall into the 40s and a late frost is not unheard of in northern New England and Minnesota into the first week of June. By late September the nights are cool and dew is heavy, and October sealing is a gamble that mostly loses. If you missed the window, wait for spring rather than forcing it.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mid-Atlantic and Ohio Valley</h3>

        <p>
          Early May through early October. July and August are hot and humid enough that mornings are essential, and afternoon thunderstorms are a daily risk, so check the radar not just the forecast. September is usually the best month of the year here: warm surfaces, cooler nights that are still above 50°F, and a long dry spell more often than not.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Southeast and Gulf Coast</h3>

        <p>
          Two windows: March through May, and late September through November. Midsummer is technically possible but the combination of surface temperatures over 130°F, dew points in the 70s and afternoon storms makes it miserable and unreliable. Spring is our preference because the pavement has been through winter and the sealer gets a full summer to harden before the next cold spell.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Southern Plains and Texas</h3>

        <p>
          March to May and October to November. Summer surfaces in Dallas and Oklahoma City can pass 140°F and there is no misting trick that makes that workable for a homeowner with a squeegee. Watch spring storm systems and give yourself a three-day dry forecast.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mountain West</h3>

        <p>
          June through mid-September, with a sharp eye on overnight lows. At altitude the day-night swing is enormous; a 80°F afternoon in Denver or Salt Lake can be followed by a 45°F night in June. Seal on the first day of a warm spell, not the last, and favour July and August when nights are reliably mild. Low humidity means fast drying, so misting and morning work matter more than they do in the East.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Pacific Northwest</h3>

        <p>
          July through September, full stop. Everything outside that window carries a real risk of rain within the 24-hour cure period, and the marine air keeps humidity high even on dry days. Late July and August are dependable. Pick a stretch with three dry days in a row.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Southwest and Desert</h3>

        <p>
          October through April, avoiding the monsoon season and the midsummer surfaces that can hit 150°F in Phoenix. Winter days in the 60s and 70s with dry air and mild nights are ideal, which is the reverse of the calendar everyone else uses. Check for overnight lows near 40°F in December and January in higher desert areas.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you want the broader picture of month-by-month timing rather than temperature alone, see <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="forecast" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          8. Reading the Forecast and Surviving a Cold Snap
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to look at</h3>

        <p>
          Open an hourly forecast, not the daily summary. You need five things: the temperature at your planned start time, the high, the low for the next two nights, the hourly precipitation probability for the next 36 hours, and the dew point. A daily summary that says "78 and sunny" hides a 44°F low and a 60 per cent chance of showers at 4 a.m.
        </p>

        <p>
          Treat any precipitation probability above about 30 per cent within the first 12 hours as a no, and above 50 per cent within 24 hours as a no. Sealer is not expensive enough to justify a coin toss. Check the radar as well, because summer storms in the East and Midwest often appear as a low daily percentage that turns into a very wet twenty minutes over your street.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then look two days out. You want the cure period, not just the application day, to be warm and dry. A hot Saturday followed by a cold front on Sunday is worse than a mild Saturday followed by a mild Sunday.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">If a cold snap hits mid-cure</h3>

        <p>
          Forecasts are wrong, and sometimes you have sealed on a 70°F Saturday and the Sunday night forecast has dropped to 38°F. What you do depends on how far along the cure is.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the coat has had a full warm sunny day and is dry to the touch by evening, most of the coalescence has happened and a single cool night in the low 40s will usually not ruin it. Keep everything off it, do not water the lawn next to it, and let the next warm day finish the job. Expect to lose a little durability; you may be resealing a year sooner than planned.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the coat is only a few hours old, still tacky, and frost is possible, you have a problem with no clean solution. Covering a driveway with tarps traps moisture and prints the tarp texture into the film, so we do not recommend it. Do not try to warm it with anything. The honest answer is to leave it, see what it looks like after two warm days, and be prepared for the coat to powder in places that then need a light scrub and a second coat when the weather returns. A first coat that partly failed and was topped correctly is not a disaster; it just cost a second pail.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the coat actually freezes before it has dried, the section is lost. It will look grey and dusty once it thaws. Wait for warm weather, pressure wash the loose material off, and reseal. This is a large part of why we tell people the pail's 50°F minimum should be read as 55°F with a warm night behind it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Product dry and cure times in each of these scenarios are covered in more detail in <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">how long driveway sealer takes to dry</a>, and if the coat has stayed tacky for days there is a separate explanation in <a href="/driveway-sealer-still-sticky/">why driveway sealer is still sticky</a>.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Checking the daytime high and ignoring the overnight low</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The day gets it dry. The night decides whether it cures. A 72°F afternoon followed by a 41°F night is a failed coat waiting to happen.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sealing at two in the afternoon in July</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">The air says 88°F; the pavement says 130°F. The sealer flashes before it levels and every squeegee pass leaves a permanent line. Start at dawn or wait for a cooler day.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Trusting the air temperature on a shaded spring driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Pavement in the shade in April can be 8 to 10°F colder than the forecast. Point an infrared thermometer at the coldest corner before you open the pail.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Squeezing in one more coat in late October</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Cool days, heavy dew and cold nights mean the emulsion never properly coalesces. It looks fine in November and powders off under the plough in January.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Flooding the surface instead of misting it in hot weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Puddles thin the sealer and leave pale, weak patches in every low spot. Damp and dark is the goal; standing water is a mistake.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the minimum temperature to seal a driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Most asphalt emulsion and coal tar sealers need 50°F and rising for both the air and the pavement, and the temperature must stay above 50°F for at least 24 hours afterwards. The ideal range is 55 to 85°F with low humidity and a light breeze.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a driveway in 90 degree weather?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">You can, but the pavement will be 110 to 130°F in full sun and the sealer will flash-dry before it levels, leaving streaks and a weak film. Work early in the morning, lightly mist the surface first, and stay out of the afternoon sun. Above 95°F air temperature, wait for a cooler day.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the surface temperature matter more than air temperature?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes. The sealer cures on the pavement, not in the air. Black asphalt in direct sun can run 20 to 40°F hotter than the air, while a shaded driveway on a cool morning can sit well below air temperature. An infrared thermometer pointed at the surface tells you the number that actually counts.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if it gets too cold after sealing a driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Water-based emulsions cure by evaporation and coalescence, and both stall below about 50°F. If the film drops near freezing before it has cured, the emulsion breaks and the sealer never forms a proper film. It stays soft, powders, or peels the following spring.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How cold can it get at night after sealing a driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Keep the overnight low above 50°F for the first night and ideally the first two. A 45°F night after a warm day will usually survive if the day was dry and sunny, but anything approaching 40°F on the first night is a genuine risk, and frost is a failure.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does humidity affect driveway sealer drying?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Enormously. Water-based sealer dries by losing water to the air, and humid air takes it slowly. At 85 per cent humidity a coat that would be dry to touch in 4 hours can take 12, and heavy dew overnight can rewet a film that was not fully cured and leave it blotchy.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to seal a driveway in the morning or evening?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Morning, once the dew has burned off and the surface is dry. That gives the sealer a full day of warmth and sunlight to cure before the temperature and humidity climb overnight. Evening application leaves the film to face dew and falling temperatures at its weakest.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What month is best for sealing a driveway?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">In most of the US, late May through September, avoiding the hottest and most humid weeks of July and August. Late spring and early autumn give the best combination of warm surfaces, mild nights and long dry spells. The exact month depends on your region.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

